Concurrency

Suppose we have:

1 CPU core
3 worker threads
3 client connections
Worker 1 → Client A
Worker 2 → Client B
Worker 3 → Client C

Since there is only one CPU core, only one worker thread can execute at any instant.

The OS rapidly switches between them.

CPU Core

↓

Worker 1 (Client A)

↓

Worker 2 (Client B)

↓

Worker 3 (Client C)

↓

Worker 1

↓

Worker 2

↓

Worker 3

...

Although only one thread is running at a time, all three clients make progress.

This is concurrency.

Parallelism

Now suppose we have:

3 CPU cores
8 worker threads
8 client connections
Worker 1 → Client A
Worker 2 → Client B
Worker 3 → Client C
...
Worker 8 → Client H

The OS schedules the threads across the available CPU cores.

For example:

Core 1              Core 2              Core 3

Worker 1            Worker 2            Worker 3

(Client A)          (Client B)          (Client C)

These three workers are executing at the exact same time.

When one finishes or blocks (e.g., waiting for network data), the OS may schedule another worker:

Core 1              Core 2              Core 3

Worker 4            Worker 2            Worker 5

(Client D)          (Client B)          (Client E)

So even though there are 8 threads, only 3 can execute simultaneously because there are only 3 CPU cores.

The remaining 5 threads are waiting for their turn.

This is parallelism.